{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>If your brother becomes poor and sells some of his holding.</b> This teaches that a person is not permitted to sell his field except under duress and poverty. 'Some of his holding' — and not all his holding: the Torah teaches proper conduct, that he should leave something for himself." "<b>And when his redeemer who is near to him comes and redeems what his brother sold.</b> This ruling that the Torah commanded is a way of mercy toward one who sells his field out of duress and poverty, so that he can redeem it within a fixed time — either himself, if his means suffice and he finds enough for its redemption, or one of his near relatives — so that it may return to his holding before the Jubilee. And if his means do not suffice and he has no near redeemer who can redeem it, then he must wait until the Jubilee, and it goes out in the Jubilee without payment and returns to his holding. Now, the seller of such a field cannot redeem it until after two years, because it must remain in the buyer's possession for two years so that he may eat its produce; this is what is written: ״במספר שני תבואות ימכר לך״ — 'according to the number of years of produce shall it be sold to you' — for the word שני embraces both the sense of שנים ('years') and the sense of שנים ('two'); and within these two years he cannot redeem it, but only from that point onward. And note that this is the reverse of a house, which he can redeem in the first year, as it is written: ״ימים תהיה גאולתו״ — 'its redemption shall be days'; and from that point onward he cannot redeem it. This is what it says: ״אם לא יגאל עד מלאת לו שנה תמימה וקם הבית אשר בעיר אשר לו חומה לצמיתות״ — 'if it is not redeemed until a full year has elapsed, then the house that is in a walled city shall remain permanently' (Leviticus 25:30)." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'If your brother becomes poor' — these are Israel, who grow weak from the commandments. 'And sells some of his holding' — that they are sold into the hands of their enemies, the idolaters. 'And his redeemer comes' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Jeremiah 50:34): ״גואלם חזק ה' צבאות שמו״ — 'their Redeemer is strong, YHWH of Hosts is His name.' 'Who is near to him' — as it is written (Psalms 145:18): ״קרוב ה' לכל קוראיו״ — 'HaShem is near to all who call Him.' This is what Scripture says (Hosea 5:15): ״אלך אשובה אל מקומי״ — 'I will go, I will return to My place' — it is not written here ״אלך ואשובה״ ('I will go and return'), but rather ״אלך אשובה״ ('I will go — I will return'); for had He said ״אלך ואשובה״, there would be no remedy for Israel. Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: even though I go, they do repentance and I return; therefore it is written ״אלך אשובה״. And out of the distress that comes upon them they will do repentance and seek My face, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:30): ״בצר לך ומצאוך״ — 'in your distress, all these things shall find you' — and I restore My Shekhinah to them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:32.526750222Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}